The final match of the [[1954 FIFA World Cup]] was played at the [[Wankdorf Stadium]] in [[Bern]], Switzerland, on 4 July 1954, and saw [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] beat the heavily favoured [[Golden Team]] of [[Hungary national football team|Hungary]] 3–2. Earlier in the [[1954 FIFA World Cup Group 2|group stage]], Hungary had defeated West Germany 8–3.

The 1954 final is often listed as one of the greatest matches in World Cup history, and also one of its most unexpected upsets.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2014/06/16/sport/football/world-cup-football-top-ten/|first=James|last=Masters|publisher=CNN|title=World Cup: the top 10 matches|date=16 June 2014|access-date=10 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140617225826/http://edition.cnn.com/2014/06/16/sport/football/world-cup-football-top-ten/|archive-date=17 June 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> Beyond football, some historians ascribe to the match a lasting impact on both [[History of Germany|German]] and [[History of Hungary|Hungarian]] post-World War II history, contributing in West Germany to a sense of regained international recognition after the lost [[World War II|Second World War]] and [[denazification]], and in Hungary to discontent with the communist-authoritarian regime in the run-up to the [[1956 Hungarian revolution]].<ref name ="forever"/> In Germany, the 1954 final is known as the '''Miracle of Bern''' (German: ''Wunder von Bern'', {{IPA|de|ˌvʊndɐ fɔn ˈbɛʁn|pron|De-Wunder von Bern.ogg}}).

The win earned Germany its first of four World Cup titles, with the other titles to follow in [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974]] and [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]] as West Germany, and in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]] as [[German reunification|reunified Germany]]. West Germany was the third country to win a World Cup, following Uruguay ([[1930 FIFA World Cup|1930]] and [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]]) and Italy ([[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]] and [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]]), although, different from Uruguay and Italy, West Germany won its maiden title as a guest rather than host. For Hungary, the second place in 1954 remains the best World Cup result to date, jointly with finishing runners-up in 1938. The 1954 tournament is the only FIFA World Cup thus far in which two teams from [[Central Europe]] contested the final, with another Central European team, of [[Austria national football team|Austria]], finishing third in the competition.

==Background==

===Hungary===
{{see also|Golden Team}}

Hungary's legendary ''Golden Team'' – also known as the ''Mighty Magyars'' – was the favorite to win the 1954 World Cup.<ref>{{cite web|title=Best teams never to win a World Cup: Hungary 1954|work=ESPNFC|date=6 May 2014|url=http://www.espnfc.us/blog/world-cup-central/59/post/1845212/best-teams-never-to-win-a-world-cup-hungary-1954|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220130222/http://www.espnfc.us/blog/world-cup-central/59/post/1845212/best-teams-never-to-win-a-world-cup-hungary-1954|archive-date=20 December 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> In the five years prior to the final, it had remained unbeaten in 31 games (32 if counting a match against East Germany in 1952 that is not considered an official international). Hungary was also the reigning [[Football at the 1952 Summer Olympics|Olympic Champion]] and winner of the [[Central European International Cup]] in 1953. In 1953, Hungary [[1953 England v Hungary football match|defeated England 6–3]] at Wembley, becoming the first team outside the United Kingdom and Ireland to beat England on home soil, and they [[1954 Hungary v England football match|thrashed England 7–1]] in [[Budapest]] just before the World Cup. Hungary did not have to play qualifiers for the 1954 World Cup, as opponent Poland withdrew for lack of prospects.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|p=103}}

The players of the Hungary national team were full-time professionals. Most played for the army club [[Budapest Honvéd FC]], or for [[MTK Budapest FC]], which, in the 1950s, was run by Hungary's secret police.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=54–56}} Several members of the Golden Team were well known and highly regarded for their skills, including forwards [[Sándor Kocsis]] and [[Ferenc Puskás]], attacking midfielder [[Nándor Hidegkuti]], half-back [[József Bozsik]], outside forward [[Zoltán Czibor]] and goalkeeper [[Gyula Grosics]]. Coach [[Gusztáv Sebes]], who was also Hungary's Deputy Minister for Sport, and MTK coach [[Márton Bukovi]] are credited with developing further the rigid [[Formation (association football)|WM formation]] that was standard in European football in the early 1950s.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=45, 46}} 

Innovations included pulling Hidegkuti – who was nominally listed as centre-forward – back into midfield to create space for the inside forwards, Puskás and Kocsis; employing Bozsik as deep-lying playmaker, with the other half-back, [[József Zakariás]], covering for Bozsik; requesting full-backs to assist in attack and wingers to assist in defence; and introducing flexible positional play that created confusion among opponents used to fixed roles and strict man-marking.{{sfn|Wilson|2013|pp=86–92}}

===West Germany===
The three German entities emerging from the [[Second World War]] – West Germany, East Germany and the [[Saar (protectorate)|Saar protectorate]] – were not admitted to [[FIFA]] until late 1950. As a result, Germany missed the [[1950 FIFA World Cup]]. In the early 1950s, [[Sepp Herberger]] – resuming the role as national team coach that he had already occupied between 1936 and 1942 – built the West German team around a nucleus of players from the club [[1. FC Kaiserslautern]], the German champions of 1951 and 1953, led by veteran playmaker [[Fritz Walter]].{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=93–96}} 

The players were semi-professionals (''Vertragsspieler''), who often worked in a second job or owned a business to support their incomes. Prior to the 1954 tournament, [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] had played only a few friendly internationals and a short qualifying campaign (against [[Norway national football team|Norway]] and the [[Saarland national football team|Saar]]).{{sfn|Heinrich|2004|pp=14–29}} This lack of international exposure left both national and foreign observers unclear about the quality of the German team. 

Herberger advocated clear roles in defence but permanent positional switches in attack – dubbed the "Herberger whirl" – to unseat the opponents' defenders.{{sfn|Escher|2016|pp=69–86}}

