===Hungary===
[[File:Stade de Suisse, Wankdorf with 1954 World Cup memorial.jpg|thumb|275px|A well-known photograph of the 1954 final is installed in front of the Wankdorf's successor stadium, the [[Stade de Suisse]].]]

In the first stage, Hungary and West Germany were drawn into the same group (2).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/1954switzerland|publisher=FIFA.com|title=FIFA World Cup 1954 Switzerland|access-date=15 December 2016|archive-date=14 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220414034824/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/1954switzerland|url-status=live}}</ref> Hungary opened the World Cup with a 9–0 win over South Korea. It then played Germany, winning 8–3, and therefore qualified for the quarter-finals. In the game, Puskás suffered an ankle hairline fracture from a foul by German central defender [[Werner Liebrich]] that made Puskás miss the quarter- and semi-finals.

In the quarter-finals, Hungary beat co-favorites [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] – runners-up of the 1950 World Cup – with 4–2, despite Puskás' absence. The hard-fought, grueling match became famous as the [[Battle of Berne (1954 FIFA World Cup)|Battle of Berne]]. In the semi-finals, Hungary defeated defending World Cup champions [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] 4–2 after extra time. Also this match is regarded a classic for the high quality of both sides' attacking game.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.worldsoccer.com/blogs/uruguay-v-hungary-1954-352539|publisher=World Soccer|title=World Cup memorable matches: Uruguay v Hungary 1954|date=7 June 2014 |access-date=15 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402101817/http://www.worldsoccer.com/blogs/uruguay-v-hungary-1954-352539 |archive-date=2 April 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref> Kocsis scored twice in both the quarter- and semi-finals.

From a statistical perspective, on the eve of the 1954 World Cup final, the Hungarian team held the highest [[World Football Elo Ratings#All-time highest ratings|Elo score]] for a national side to that date. Its rating was surpassed only in 2014 by Germany.

===West Germany===
West Germany first played Turkey, winning 4–1. In the subsequent 3–8 loss to Hungary, Herberger rested several key players and played others out of position (for example, Fritz Walter as central striker instead of playmaker). The main purpose was to preserve energy for the upcoming decider against (once more) Turkey. However, by sparing his strongest eleven, Herberger may have obscured the real strength of the German team to its subsequent opponents, including Hungary. Germany won the decider against Turkey 7–2.{{sfn|Heinrich|2004|pp=38, 39}}

West Germany went as outsider into both its quarter- and semi-final matches.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://web.ard.de/special/helden1954/pages/2448.php?ch=1 |publisher=web.ard.de |title=Der Weg ins Finale – erst gedemütigt, dann der Durchmarsch |language=de |access-date=15 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160627091425/http://web.ard.de/special/helden1954/pages/2448.php?ch=1 |archive-date=27 June 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the quarter-finals, West Germany beat highly regarded [[Yugoslavia national football team|Yugoslavia]] – the Olympic silver medalists of 1952 – with 2–0. The team then went on to defeat [[Austrian national football team|Austria]] in the semi-finals, with an unexpectedly lopsided score of 6–1.

==Pre-match==
[[File:Wankdorf 1954 world cup final match clock.jpg|thumb|150px|The restored match clock has been installed in front of the [[Stade de Suisse]] as a memorial.]]

===Team preparations===
During the tournament, the Hungarian team took residence in a hotel in the town center of [[Solothurn]].{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=108–11}} This had several repercussions. As Hungary's semi-final against Uruguay was decided only after extra time, the team missed the return bus from Lausanne, and had to organize private cars that arrived only late at night. The evening before the final, a village fair took place in front of the hotel that lasted until the early morning hours and disrupted the players' sleep. Further, Herberger's assistant Albert Sing checked into the hotel, from where he reported about the Hungarian team preparations.<ref>{{cite news |title=Als beim WM-Final in Bern für die Ungarn die Seifenblase platzte |url=http://www.solothurnerzeitung.ch/solothurn/stadt-solothurn/als-beim-wm-final-in-bern-fuer-die-ungarn-die-seifenblase-platzte-128171122 |work=solothurnerzeitung.ch |date=12 November 2014|language=de |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220102158/http://www.solothurnerzeitung.ch/solothurn/stadt-solothurn/als-beim-wm-final-in-bern-fuer-die-ungarn-die-seifenblase-platzte-128171122 |archive-date=20 December 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref>

By contrast, the German team resided in the tranquil lake town of [[Spiez]], where it was unaffected from such disturbances. The ''Spirit of Spiez'' became proverbial in Germany for describing the team's morale and comradeship.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=117–24}}

===Lineups and tactics===
Puskás returned to the Hungarian squad for the final, despite doubts about his fitness. Further, Sebes replaced the customary right winger [[László Budai]] with [[Mihály Tóth (footballer, born 1926)|Mihály Tóth]], as he considered Budai too exhausted after the semi-finals.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|p=138}} He also placed left winger Czibor on the right in the first half (switching with Tóth), to exploit Czibor's speed against Germany's left fullback [[Werner Kohlmeyer]].<ref name="bleibt">{{cite news |title=Wir haben verloren – das bleibt |url=http://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/jahre-nach-dem-wunder-von-bern-wir-haben-verloren-das-bleibt-1.314084 |work=sueddeutsche.de |date=11 May 2010|language=de |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160923112948/http://www.sueddeutsche.de/sport/jahre-nach-dem-wunder-von-bern-wir-haben-verloren-das-bleibt-1.314084 |archive-date=23 September 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref> Other than this, Sebes fielded his standard formation.

The German team had evolved in the course of the tournament, finding its final formation only in the semi-finals. In particular, central defender Liebrich and right winger [[Helmut Rahn]] secured places in the starting lineup only after strong performances in the quarter-finals against Yugoslavia. In the final, Herberger had Hidegkuti marked by a midfielder, [[Horst Eckel]], to prevent Hidegkuti from pulling Liebrich out of the defence when he dropped deep into midfield.{{sfn|Wilson|2013|pp=91, 92}}<ref>{{cite web |url=http://bundesligafanatic.com/interview-of-the-week-horst-eckel-on-the-miracle-of-bern-fritz-walter-and-modern-football//|first=Cristian|last=Nyari|publisher=BundesligaFanatic|title=Interview of the Week – Horst Eckel on the Miracle of Bern, Fritz Walter and Modern Football|date=10 February 2012|access-date=10 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120513182832/http://bundesligafanatic.com/interview-of-the-week-horst-eckel-on-the-miracle-of-bern-fritz-walter-and-modern-football//|archive-date=13 May 2012|url-status=usurped}}</ref> He also instructed left winger [[Hans Schäfer]] to cover Bozsik when Hungary was attacking, to help Fritz Walter – who normally would have been Bozsik's marker – preserve energy and concentrate on organising the German game.<ref>{{cite news |title=Hans Schäfer: Wir sind keine Helden von Bern |url=http://www.tagesspiegel.de/sport/fussball-wm2010/wm-finale-1954-hans-schaefer-wir-sind-keine-helden-von-bern/1868692.html |work=tagesspiegel.de |date=26 June 2010|language=de |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220150204/http://www.tagesspiegel.de/sport/fussball-wm2010/wm-finale-1954-hans-schaefer-wir-sind-keine-helden-von-bern/1868692.html |archive-date=20 December 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Conditions===
The match was played in heavy rain, conditions that the German side had christened "Fritz Walter weather",<ref>{{cite web |url=http://web.ard.de/special/helden1954/pages/2437.php?ch=1/ |title=Hoch gepokert und gewonnen |first=Markus |last=Wessel |access-date=23 August 2009 |publisher=[[ARD (broadcaster)|ARD]] |language=de |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090513011055/http://web.ard.de/special/helden1954/pages/2437.php?ch=1/ |archive-date=13 May 2009 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref> as Walter was known for playing his best football in the wet. In addition, the German team was equipped with boots supplied by [[Adidas]] that featured hitherto unknown, exchangeable, screw-in studs. The boots could be adapted to any weather, enabling the German players to wear their regular footwear despite the adverse conditions.

==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:Les deux capitaines de la finale de Coupe du monde de football 1954 (G. Walter, D. Puskas, arbitre Orlandini).jpg|thumb|150px|The captains Fritz Walter and Ferenc Puskás with referee Ling (Liebig football card from 1966)]]
Puskás put his team ahead after only six minutes, when Bozsik intercepted a pass from Liebrich and found Kocsis with a through ball. Kocsis' blocked shot fell to Puskás, and Puskás scored from close range.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=174}}<ref name="spielverlagerung"/>{{sfn|Puskás|1955|pp=173–76}}<ref name="FIFAClip">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/videos/y=2014/m=7/video=60-years-ago-today-1954-world-cup-final-fr-germany-3-2-hungary-2398675.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150610133402/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/videos/y=2014/m=7/video=60-years-ago-today-1954-world-cup-final-fr-germany-3-2-hungary-2398675.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 June 2015 |title=WC 1954 World Cup Final: FR Germany 3–2 Hungary |website=FIFA.com }}</ref> Czibor added a second goal for Hungary a mere two minutes later, exploiting a misunderstanding in the German defense: Kohlmeyer secured a through ball from Bozsik but wrong-footed goalkeeper [[Toni Turek]] with a back pass, who failed to control the ball. Czibor sprinted in between and put the ball into the empty net.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=175}}

West Germany came back quickly. In the 10th minute, Fritz Walter played Rahn free on the left wing, who crossed low and hard into the center. The ball went through the legs of right fullback [[Jenő Buzánszky]], Zakariás lunged for the ball but could only deflect it, and forward [[Max Morlock]] converted from close range.<ref name="FIFAClip"/>{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=176}} Eight minutes later Morlock dribbled through the Hungarian defense, but center back [[Gyula Lóránt]] cleared for a corner. Fritz Walter played the corner short, Buzánszky cleared for another corner. The second corner kick came long and high, finding Rahn, who leveled the score.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=177}}

[[File:Fussball-Weltmeisterschaft 1954 (Com M03-0108-005-0025).jpg|thumb|The Hungarians celebrating their first goal]]
Hungary took control after the equalizer and came to several good chances. In the 23rd minute, left fullback [[Mihály Lantos]] chipped the ball into the box, Kocsis headed on to Hidegkuti, but Hidegkuti's volley was saved spectacularly by Turek.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=178}}{{sfn|Raupp|2019|pp=108–109}} Four minutes later, Hidegkuti fired another shot from the edge of the penalty box that hit the goal post.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=179}} Only towards the end of the first half did West Germany come to more chances. In the 42nd minute, Fritz Walter found Schäfer in the penalty box, who dribbled past Lóránt and forced a save at full stretch from Grosics. The loose ball fell to Rahn, but Buzánszky cleared on the goal line.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=179}}

Hungary came out attacking in the second half and created plentiful chances. Two minutes after the restart, Bozsik played Puskás free in the penalty box, but Puskás fired the ball straight at Turek.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=182}} In the 54th minute, Kohlmeyer cleared twice on the goal line from Tóth,{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=183}} and three minutes later Kocsis headed a cross by Tóth against the cross bar.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=184}} 

In the 67th minute, Turek saved a shot by Puskás with his foot,{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=185}} and in the 78th minute he left the goal line to block a through ball from Bozsik ahead of the onrushing Czibor. Hidegkuti picked up the loose ball but shot into the side netting.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=185}} West Germany's first good chance of the second half came in the 72nd minute when Fritz Walter played Rahn free after a corner kick, and Rahn's powerful shot from the edge of the penalty box was saved by Grosics.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=185}}

With six minutes left, Schäfer dispossessed Bozsik and played a high cross into the penalty box. Rahn picked up Lantos' short clearance, feinted a pass to center forward [[Ottmar Walter]] that wrong-footed the Hungarian defenders, moved into the penalty box, and drove the ball hard and low past Grosics for the third German goal.<ref name="FIFAClip"/>{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=187}} Two minutes later, Puskás appeared to have leveled the score once more when he converted a through ball from Tóth flicked on by Kocsis, but the attempt was ruled offside.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=188}} In the final minute of the game, Czibor had another chance to equalize, but his shot from close range was again stopped by Turek.{{sfn|Walter|1954|p=189}}

The whistle soon thereafter ended not only the match, but also the Golden Team's unbeaten run.

