A match analysis by the website ''spielverlagerung.de'', produced 60 years after the game based on data extracted from [[Herbert Zimmermann (football commentator)|Herbert Zimmermann]]'s live radio commentary, shows Hungary ahead in most categories, except goals scored.<ref name="spielverlagerung">{{cite web |url=http://spielverlagerung.de/2014/06/06/liveanalyse-des-wm-finals-1954///|first=Tobias|last=Escher|publisher=spielverlagerung.de|title=Liveanalyse des WM-Finals 1954 |date=6 June 2014|access-date=15 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140612203652/http://spielverlagerung.de/2014/06/06/liveanalyse-des-wm-finals-1954///|archive-date=12 June 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> Hungarian superiority was especially pronounced during minutes 20–40 and 45–70, while West Germany had good phases towards the end of the first and second halves.

The analysis also suggests that many Hungarian attacks went through the centre, in most cases orchestrated by Bozsik, who played numerous accurate through balls to Hungary's forwards. By contrast, the German team attacked almost exclusively from the wings, with Fritz Walter playing a key role as ball distributor and flexible offensive player generating numerical superiority in select portions of the pitch.

Further, the data point to an excellent defensive performance by Liebrich, who – according to Zimmermann's commentary – blocked six shots, intercepted nine passes, and won all of his 10 one-on-one duels.

==Controversies==

===Refereeing decisions===
Several calls by the English referee [[William Ling (referee)|William Ling]] came under scrutiny after the final. These included:

* ''Germany's second goal.'' Grosics attempted to clear the corner kick by Fritz Walter in the air, but collided in the six-yard box with Schäfer. As a result, the ball went through to Rahn, who converted. Had Ling called a foul on Grosics, the goal would not have counted.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://longballfootball.wordpress.com/2013/09/11/historical-football-2-das-wunder-von-bern |title=Historical Football #2 – Das Wunder von Bern |publisher=The Long Ball |date=11 September 2013 |access-date=15 December 2016 |archive-date=22 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222090123/https://longballfootball.wordpress.com/2013/09/11/historical-football-2-das-wunder-von-bern/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
* ''Hungary's disallowed third goal.'' Eyewitness accounts differ on whether Puskás was offside. The official television footage allows no clear verdict, as it fails to show Puskás' position at the moment when the ball was passed. The Hungarian players claimed that Ling first wanted to allow the goal, but changed his mind after talking to Welsh linesman [[Sandy Griffiths]].{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=205}}

===Doping allegations===
Immediately after the match, rumors emerged that the German team had taken performance-enhancing substances. Several members of the team fell ill with [[jaundice]], presumably from a contaminated needle, and of them, winger [[Richard Herrmann]], died just eight years later of [[cirrhosis]], at the age of 39.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/sports/soccer/Doping-Study-Throws-Shadow-Over-Germanys-Success.html |title=Doping Study Throws Shadow Over Germany's Success |last=Hughes |first=Rob |website=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |date=6 August 2013 |access-date=30 December 2022 |archive-date=29 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191129135009/https://www.nytimes.com/2013/08/07/sports/soccer/Doping-Study-Throws-Shadow-Over-Germanys-Success.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Members of the team later claimed they had been injected [[glucose]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Das Wunder von Bern: Tor-Rekord und Doping-Verdacht |url=http://www.sueddeutsche.de/news/sport/fussball-das-wunder-von-bern-tor-rekord-und-doping-verdacht-dpa.urn-newsml-dpa-com-20090101-140325-99-01581 |work=sueddeutsche.de |date=3 May 2014 |language=de |access-date=14 February 2015 |archive-date=29 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150929061107/http://www.sueddeutsche.de/news/sport/fussball-das-wunder-von-bern-tor-rekord-und-doping-verdacht-dpa.urn-newsml-dpa-com-20090101-140325-99-01581 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and the team physician Franz Loogen said in 2004 that the players had only been given [[Vitamin C]] before the game.<ref>{{cite web|title=Erzürnte Weltmeister: "Vitamin C, sonst nichts"|work=Spiegel Online|date=31 March 2004|url=http://www.spiegel.de/sport/fussball/erzuernte-weltmeister-vitamin-c-sonst-nichts-a-293370.html|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130806040117/http://www.spiegel.de/sport/fussball/erzuernte-weltmeister-vitamin-c-sonst-nichts-a-293370.html|archive-date=6 August 2013|url-status=live}}</ref> 

In 2010, {{ill|Erik Eggers|de}} of the [[Humboldt University of Berlin]] challenged the validity of these claims, stating: "Vitamin C is not injected. This is very unusual. They could have just eaten an orange instead."<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/oct/27/west-germany-1954-drugs-study |title=West Germany's 1954 World Cup win may have been drug-fuelled, says study |website=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |via=[[Reuters]] |date=27 October 2010 |access-date=30 December 2022 |quote="The most important indication, though, is that vitamin C is not injected. This is very unusual. They could have just eaten an orange instead." |archive-date=5 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131005154628/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/oct/27/west-germany-1954-drugs-study |url-status=live }}</ref> As the co-author of a study conducted by the [[University of Leipzig]], he also hypothesized that the German players, unbeknownst to them, may have been injected with Pervitin ([[methamphetamine]]) – a stimulant given to soldiers in World War II.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/2010-10-26-3288531362_x.htm |work=[[USA Today]] |title=Researcher raises doping spectre over 1954 WCup |date=26 October 2010 |access-date=21 July 2014 |archive-date=30 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110430015759/http://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/2010-10-26-3288531362_x.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2013, the ''[[Süddeutsche Zeitung]]'' publicized details from the finished, 800-page study, in which Eggers and his team stated that the use of [[amphetamine]]s became "normal" in German football from the end of the 1940s, and reaffirmed their previous claim that an unspecified number of players of the 1954 team received injections of Pervitin.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://usatoday.com/story/sports/olympics/2013/08/03/report-west-germany-systematically-doped-athletes-olympics/2615323/ |title=Report: West Germany systematically doped athletes |work=USA Today |language=en-US |location=Berlin |date=3 August 2013 |access-date=31 December 2022 |via=[[Associated Press]] |archive-date=31 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221231140230/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/olympics/2013/08/03/report-west-germany-systematically-doped-athletes-olympics/2615323/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In an interview with ''[[Der Spiegel]]'', Eggers also claimed that the [[German Football Association]] hampered their research by not allowing the team to access their archives.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.spiegel.de/international/germany/interview-with-erik-eggers-about-doping-in-west-german-sports-a-916988.html |title='They Blocked Our Work' |website=[[Der Spiegel (website)|Spiegel International]] |translator=Ella Ornstein |date=16 August 2013 |access-date=31 December 2022 |archive-date=31 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221231140207/https://www.spiegel.de/international/germany/interview-with-erik-eggers-about-doping-in-west-german-sports-a-916988.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In any case, doping was not illegal in 1954, with doping controls introduced by FIFA only in 1966.<ref>{{cite web|title=A brief history of doping|work=fifa.com|url=https://www.fifa.com/who-we-are/news/brief-history-doping-514062|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122214944/https://www.fifa.com/who-we-are/news/brief-history-doping-514062|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 November 2020}}</ref>

==Radio commentaries==
As television was available only in a few homes or public places in 1954, the radio reports by [[György Szepesi]] and [[Herbert Zimmermann (football commentator)|Herbert Zimmermann]] were the main vehicles for transmitting the World Cup final live to the Hungarian and West German populations. Both commentaries became legendary for their passionate, emotional reporting styles, and are sometimes credited with contributing to the charged atmosphere in both countries after the final.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=10–14}}{{sfn|Oswald|2006|pp=369, 370}}

Szepesi – who later became the chairman of the [[Hungarian Football Federation]] – had been a household name in Hungary since his report of the Golden Team's 6–3 victory over England in 1953.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.pastemagazine.com/articles/2016/12/6-3-inside-the-budapest-bar-named-for-hungarys-mos.html |title=6–3 – Inside the Budapest Bar Named for Hungary's Most Famous Football Result |first=Peterjon |last=Creswell |publisher=Paste Magazine |date=22 December 2016 |access-date=22 January 2017 |archive-date=2 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202013141/https://www.pastemagazine.com/articles/2016/12/6-3-inside-the-budapest-bar-named-for-hungarys-mos.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He started his commentary of the World Cup final with cautious confidence, reinforced after Hungary's opening goal: "Puskás shoots... GOAL... A WONDERFUL GOAL! ... There is the lead. Öcsi's [i.e., Puskas'] foot is fine, this one could see with this shot. ... And now: calmly, boys, just as we played at the 8–3". Doubts started creeping in after Germany shortened the lead – "the clouds are piling up in front of the mountains... hopefully this is not a bad sign" – and grew stronger at half time – "it will be even more difficult than the matches before, more difficult perhaps than against Brazil and Uruguay".{{quote without source|date=August 2023}}

Szepesi's doubts gave way to desperation with Germany's winning goal – "dear listeners, Rahn's shot is in the goal, in the right corner, six minutes before the end... The boys are standing there, distraught. The crowd screams. Six more minutes. The Hungarian team has played brilliantly – I cannot say anything else. My tears are flowing, but believe me, the boys gave it all." – and Puskás disallowed equalizer – "Puskás, you have the ball!!! SHOT!!! GOAL!!! ... OFFSIDE, offside; Griffiths indicated offside, my listeners, Griffiths has given offside – oh what a pity... what a pity". At the final whistle, Szepesi was fighting for composure: "end of the game. West Germany's team is World Champion. Dear listeners, it is hard for me to say anything, believe me... But let's recognize that the West Germans were very fair and won deservedly. ... We have been unbeaten for four years. Now we have lost. This is not a tragedy, my listeners... this is a defeat in a football match".{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=13, 15, 43, 103, 135}}

By contrast, Zimmermann kept expectations low at the outset, and continued in this vein after Hungary took the lead: "this is a great day, this is a proud day – let's not be so presumptuous to think it would have to end successfully". A measure of pride entered his voice after Germany's first – "today it is no 3–8. Today it is not a B-team. Today Germany's strongest squad is playing" – and second goal – "we have equalized, against Hungary, the technically most gifted squad that one knows!". Self-esteem grew as the match progressed – "six minutes still to play in Bern in the first half. Score: 2–2. This is more than what we dared to hope in our wildest dreams" – including after Buzánszky cleared Rahn's shot on the goal line: "now, dear Hungarians, we have to say: now you have been lucky". When Turek stopped Hidegkuti's volley from close range, Zimmermann famously called him a "god" and "devil" in the same sentence: "shot – saved by Turek! Turek, you are a dare-devil, Turek, you are a football god!".{{sfn|Raupp|2019|pp=109, 126–128}}

Proverbial in Germany are Zimmermann's description of the winning goal – "Bozsik, time and again Bozsik, the right half-back of the Hungarians, with the ball. He has lost the ball this time, to Schäfer... Schäfer has crossed, header... cleared. From the deep Rahn ought to shoot – Rahn shoots – GOAL!! GOAL!! GOAL!! GOAL!! ... Goal for Germany! ... Call me crazy, call me mental, I believe also football laymen should have a heart!" – and his jubilation at the end of the match: "Over! Over! Over! ... Over! The game is over!".{{sfn|Oswald|2006|pp=380}}{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=11, 12, 73}} Zimmermann also complimented the Hungarian team: "Next to them stand the Hungarians... the Hungarians, calm, collected. Kudos to these boys, who can lose so gracefully, who have demonstrated to be true World Champions: when they once get beaten, they carry the defeat with dignity."{{quote without source|date=August 2023}}

==Post-match and aftermath==

===Reaction in Hungary===
The loss came at a shock to Hungarian public. Spontaneous demonstrations erupted in Budapest and were directed not only against the team, but also the communist-authoritarian regime controlled by general secretary [[Mátyás Rákosi]], which had used the Golden Team's prestige to boost its own reputation.{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=142–4}} Goalkeeper Grosics characterized the post-match atmosphere in Hungary as follows:

{{Blockquote|"''The reaction in Hungary was terrible. Hundreds of thousands of people poured into the streets in the hours after the match. On the pretext of football, they demonstrated against the regime ... in those demonstrations, I believe, lay the seeds of the 1956 uprising.''"<ref name="forever">{{cite web |url=http://www.thehardtackle.com/2012/a-game-that-changed-germany-and-hungary-forever-the-miracle-of-bern//|title=The miracle of Bern – a game that changed Germany and Hungary forever|first=Amlan|last=Majundar|publisher=theHardTackle|date=11 October 2012|access-date=7 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121014011141/http://www.thehardtackle.com/2012/a-game-that-changed-germany-and-hungary-forever-the-miracle-of-bern//|archive-date=14 October 2012|url-status=live}}</ref>}}

Sebes was severely criticized for the team's selection and tactics, and blamed the negative reaction in part on Szepesi's emotional radio report. Sebes achieved that Szepesi was temporarily removed from commenting football matches for the [[Magyar Rádió|Hungarian State Radio]].{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=137–41}} The authorities revoked player privileges, such as the tacit approval of smuggling Western goods back to Hungary. Grosics – who some Hungarians blamed for the third German goal – was accused of espionage and treason, put temporarily under house arrest, and transferred against his will from [[Budapest Honvéd FC]] to the provincial team [[FC Tatabánya]].{{sfn|Kasza|2004|pp=147–50}}

===Reaction in Germany===
The unexpected win evoked a wave of euphoria throughout Germany, which suffered from a lack of international recognition in the aftermath of World War II, and where expressions of national pride were still tainted with the recent past.<ref>Schiller, Kay: Siegen für Deutschland? Patriotism, nationalism and the Germany national football team, 1954–2014. In: Historical Social Research 40 (2015), 4, pp. 176–196. URN: https://dx.doi.org/10.12759/hsr.40.2015.4.176-196; (full text in English) http://www.gesis.org/hsr/abstr/40-4/11schiller/ {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170425205105/http://www.gesis.org/hsr/abstr/40-4/11schiller/ |date=25 April 2017 }}</ref><ref>Diethelm Blecking: The 1954 "miracle of Bern": the political instrumentalization of a legend. In: Historical Social Research 40 (2015), 4, pp. 197–208. URN: https://dx.doi.org/10.12759/hsr.40.2015.4.197-208 (full text in German, abstract in English) http://www.gesis.org/hsr/abstr/40-4/12blecking/ {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170425205225/http://www.gesis.org/hsr/abstr/40-4/12blecking/ |date=25 April 2017 }}.</ref> Some publicists described the 1954 victory as a turning point in [[History of Germany since 1945|post-war German history]], notably Arthur Heinrich<ref>{{cite web|url=http://users.polisci.wisc.edu/schatzberg/ps616/Heinrich2003.pdf|title=The 1954 Soccer World Cup and the Federal Republic of Germany's Self-Discovery|first=Arthur|last=Heinrich|publisher=Sage Publications|access-date=20 June 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150506232943/http://users.polisci.wisc.edu/schatzberg/ps616/Heinrich2003.pdf|archive-date=6 May 2015|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref>{{sfn|Heinrich|2004|pp=169–79}} and [[Joachim Fest]]. In Fest's words:

{{Blockquote|"''It was a kind of liberation for the Germans from all the things that weighed down upon them after the Second World War ... July 4, 1954 is in certain aspects the founding day of the German Republic.''"<ref>{{cite web |url=http://edition.cnn.com/2011/SPORT/football/01/05/iraq.asia.six.games/|title=Five games that changed the world|first=James|last=Montague|publisher=CNN|date=13 January 2011|access-date=20 June 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140729034347/http://edition.cnn.com/2011/SPORT/football/01/05/iraq.asia.six.games/|archive-date=29 July 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref>}}

This World Cup final was also the first time since the [[World War II|Second World War]] that the [[Deutschlandlied|Germany national anthem]] was played at a global sporting event.{{sfn|Heinrich|2004|pp=100–03}}

===''Mighty Magyars'' and ''Heroes of Bern'' after 1954===
Hungary's Golden Team remained largely intact until the failed [[Hungarian Revolution of 1956]], except that coach Sebes was replaced with Bukovi in early 1956. After the revolution, Puskás, Czibor and Kocsis emigrated and, ultimately, continued their careers in Spain, playing for [[Real Madrid]] (Puskás) and [[FC Barcelona]] (Czibor and Kocsis), respectively. At the [[1958 FIFA World Cup]], only four players from the Mighty Magyars still featured – Bozsik, Budai, Grosics, Hidegkuti (Grosics would additionally play at the [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962 World Cup]] as captain of the team) – but were unable to repeat earlier success. In the 1960s and beyond, Hidegkuti, Puskás, and Lóránt became prominent international coaches, managing clubs such as [[Panathinaikos F.C.|Panathinaikos]] (Puskás), [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] (Hidegkuti), and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] (Lóránt). Lóránt's admission to the [[German Sport University Cologne]] was arranged by Herberger.<ref>{{cite web|title=Er entdeckte den totalen Fussball|author=Gyula Lóránt|work=Der Spiegel|date=18 March 1977|url=http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/d-40941673.html |access-date=13 December 2016 |language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150411122117/http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/d-40941673.html|archive-date=11 April 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> Several other Golden Team members had success as coaches in the domestic [[Nemzeti Bajnokság I|Hungarian league]].

The West German players became instantaneously famous in Germany as the ''Heroes of Bern''.{{sfn|Heinrich|2004|pp=87–94}} Several received lucrative offers to play abroad, but none accepted, continuing instead as semi-professionals in German clubs. Herberger overhauled the national team after the tournament. Hence, of the winning 1954 side, only Eckel, Rahn, Schäfer, and a 37-year old Fritz Walter still played at the 1958 World Cup, reaching the semi-finals, and Schäfer would also play in 1962. Few of the 1954 World Champions went into coaching; an exception is Liebrich, who is credited with saving his home club Kaiserslautern from [[Bundesliga]] relegation in 1965 – with Lóránt becoming Liebrich's successor in the following season. Some players had difficulties to cope with fame, and struggled at times with economic problems and alcoholism.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.das-wunder-von-bern.de/helden_rahn.htm|title=Helmut Rahn: der Boss|publisher=das-wunder-von-bern.de|access-date=13 December 2016|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060614135705/http://www.das-wunder-von-bern.de/helden_rahn.htm|archive-date=14 June 2006|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.das-wunder-von-bern.de/helden_kohlmeyer.htm|title=Werner Kohlmeyer: Fussballer & Leichtathlet|publisher=das-wunder-von-bern.de|access-date=13 December 2016|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060614140920/http://www.das-wunder-von-bern.de/helden_kohlmeyer.htm|archive-date=14 June 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 1990, after the fall of the [[Iron Curtain]], the [[German Football Association]] invited the surviving members of Hungary's 1954 team to join celebrations for Fritz Walter's 70th birthday.<ref name="bleibt"/> In the years that followed, both teams organized annual get-togethers, which Grosics described as follows:

{{Blockquote|"''I believe the friendship between the former Hungarian and German players can be called the world's most extraordinary. We met regularly over years and decades, alternately in Hungary and in Germany. I believe that these friendships were formed not only by the sport, but that high regard for the human beings also played a part. Between us such a relationship developed which, as I would like to stress once more, is unthinkable among national team players.''"<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.tyskforlaget.dk/WundervonBern/geschlagene_ungarn/grosics-ungarn.htm|title=Grosics: Waren überzeugt, die WM zu gewinnen|publisher=Tyskforlaget|access-date=9 December 2016|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140717201629/http://www.tyskforlaget.dk/WundervonBern/geschlagene_ungarn/grosics-ungarn.htm|archive-date=17 July 2014|url-status=live}}</ref>}}

In January 2015, the last surviving of the Golden Hungarian team, Jenő Buzánszky, died. The last surviving player from the match, Horst Eckel, died on 3 December 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|url = https://ovalmauls.wordpress.com/2018/07/12/last-survivors-of-each-world-cup-final-1930-54/|title = Last survivors of each World Cup final (1930–54)|date = 12 July 2018|access-date = 17 December 2019|archive-date = 17 December 2019|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191217230159/https://ovalmauls.wordpress.com/2018/07/12/last-survivors-of-each-world-cup-final-1930-54/|url-status = live}}</ref>

